Asking

Flags:
      --ask NAME=QUESTION       Ask a named question. Repeat for several.
      --pick OPTIONS            Choose from comma separated options.
      --rate LEVELS             Rate against levels from lowest to highest.
  -f, --file FILE               Load saved questions, a request body or a built in
                                starter set.
      --for ID                  Select a question from -f by ID, so the --pick, --rate,
                                --desc and --sep after it apply to that question.
                                Repeatable.
      --desc KEY=TEXT           Describe an option, level, yes or no.
      --sep SEP                 Set the separator for the --pick and --rate that follow
                                it.
      --box                     Make this a box question, answered with the left, top,
                                right and bottom edges of what it finds in an image,
                                each from 0 to 1. Takes no value. Experimental on
                                TypeSafe's side.
      --replace                 Let --ask override an id from -f.

How they work together:
  A positional question has the id answer. By default a question asks for a yes or no
  probability. Use --pick for a choice, --rate for ordered levels, or --box for image
  edges. A rate score is the level index averaged over p, from 0, and norm is score over
  the top index, from 0 to 1. Box edges are left, top, right and bottom, each from 0 to
  1. Box is experimental on TypeSafe's side. See Input for image restrictions.

  Question flags apply to the preceding --ask or --for. Flags before any group apply to
  the one question asked, whether positional, a lone --ask or a file with one question.
  Beside several questions they need --for ID. --ask NAME=QUESTION opens a named group
  and may repeat. --for ID selects a question from --file. Root runs issue a notice for
  each setting overriding a selected file question. Repeated --pick adds options and
  repeated --rate adds levels after earlier ones. Together they replace the file
  question's options or levels. --desc KEY=TEXT replaces only that option, level, yes or
  no description. A rate needs every level described or none. --sep changes the
  separator for following --pick and --rate. --for may repeat and keeps the wording and
  settings that no flag changes. It needs a file and an existing id and refuses
  --replace. Flags cannot change a file question shape.

  --file accepts a question file, a request body, a path or a saved name. Saved names
  are looked up in .onesie/questions, then the config directory, then built in starter
  sets. --ask needs --replace to replace an id from the file. This replaces the
  question, while --for changes selected settings.

  A question that begins with a dash needs -- before it, with all flags first, as in
  onesie -o json -- '-is this urgent'. Use the same escape for a one word question close
  to a command name, since onesie questoins exits 2 and suggests questions. On
  calibration, --for accepts only --pick, --rate, --desc and --sep. Box questions cannot
  be calibrated.

